  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES,\nNEW DELHI TROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO 25\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 19.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 25 FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN\n(THE BAY OF BENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168\nHOURS ISSUED AT 1500 UTC OF 19.11.2026 BASED ON 1200 UTC OF\n19.11.2026.\nSub: Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay\nof Bengal [Cyclone Warning for North Andhra Pradesh & Yanam and South Odisha\ncoasts]\nThe Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay of\nBengal moved northwards with the speed of 13 kmph during past 06 hours and lay centered\nat 1200 UTC, the 19th November, 2026 over the same region, near Latitude 16.1\u00b0N and\nLongitude 85.2\u00b0E, about 270 km south-southeast of Kalingapatnam (43105, Andhra\nPradesh), 350 km south of Gopalpur (43049,Odisha), 380 km east of Narsapur\n(43187,Andhra Pradesh), 420 km south of Puri (43053,Odisha) and 640 km east-northeast\nof Chennai (43279,Tamil Nadu).\nIt is likely to move north-northwestward and cross North Andhra Pradesh and South Odisha\ncoasts close to Gopalpur, by 0600 UTC of 20th November, 2026 with the maximum\nsustained wind speed of 90-100 kmph gusting to 110 kmph.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak:\nCloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very\nIntense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of depression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-\n66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. surface disturbance\n0E) wind speed (Kmph)\n19.11.26/1200 16.1/85.2 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n19.11.26/1800 17.1/85.1 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0000 18.2/85.0 90-100 gusting to 110 Severe Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0600 19.3/85.0 80-90 gusting to 100 Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/1800 20.4/85.0 50-60 gusting to 70 Deep Depression\n21.11.26/0000 22.2/85.2 20-30 gusting to 40 Well Marked Low\nPressure Area\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex (AKVAN) lay over the Westcentral Bay of Bengal and its\nneighbourhood centered near 15.9N/85.4E. Intensity of the system is characterized as\nT3.0. Associated scattered to broken low and medium-level clouds, with embedded intense\nto very intense convection lay over North and Central Bay, North Odisha, Gangetic West\nBengal, north Costal Andhra Pradesh adjoining South Odisha (minimum Cloud-Top\nTemperature (CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -90\u00b0C) and moderate to intense convection over\nsouth Bay, Jharkhand, east Bihar, Sub-Himalayan West Bengal, Sikkim, Northeast states\nof Bangladesh and Bhutan (minimum CTT) ranges from -40\u00b0C to -70\u00b0C).\nThe estimated central pressure is 998 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed is 50 kt\ngusting to 60 kt. High to very high Sea condition is prevailing over Bay of Bengal.\nRemarks:\nMadden Julian Oscillation (MJO) is in phase 4 with amplitude more than 1. It is likely to\nphase 4 till 21st November. Thus, MJO is favorable for enhancement of convective activity\nover Bay of Bengal till 21st November. However, the decreasing amplitude of the MJO\nwould be reduci",
